part two: exact enumeration on random cost tables, two cost dimensions
checks the bound holds and is not vacuous, over 400 random tables each
 regions   arms       sections      bound   max seen  mean seen
       6      5          15625         61         12       6.18
       6      6          46656         91         11       6.55
       8      4          65536         49         12       7.13
      10      5        9765625        101         15       9.56
      12      6     2176782336        181         18      12.08

no table exceeded the bound in any trial, and every observed count is
orders below the section count. So the finding P1 measured on committed
data is what the shape of the question forces, and the committed data is
one instance of it rather than the reason for it.

What this does NOT say. It does not say the weighting reading is right and
the section reading is wrong. It says they are different propositions with
a quantified gap, so an agreement recorded between them is an agreement
between two claims rather than two derivations of one claim.
